        {[
          { q: "Do I need a lawyer to review my UGC contract?", a: "For your first few contracts, a legal review is worth the investment. An entertainment or intellectual property attorney can spot problematic clauses you might miss. Once you have reviewed several contracts and understand the standard terms, you can handle routine agreements yourself. But any contract involving perpetual rights, significant exclusivity, or fees above $5,000 warrants professional review regardless of your experience level." },
          { q: "What if the brand refuses to sign a contract?", a: "Walk away. A brand that will not put terms in writing is either disorganized or intentionally avoiding accountability. Neither scenario ends well for the creator. Even a simple one-page agreement covering deliverables, payment, and usage rights is better than nothing. If a brand pushes back on formal contracts for small projects, propose a brief email summary of agreed terms with both parties confirming in writing." },
          { q: "Can I use the same contract template for every project?", a: "You can use a base template, but you should customize the scope, usage rights, payment, and timeline for each project. A one-size-fits-all contract either leaves out important project-specific details or includes irrelevant clauses that confuse the other party. Start with a solid template and adjust the variable sections for each new engagement." },
          { q: "How do I handle a brand that wants to extend usage rights after the contract period ends?", a: "This is a good problem to have — it means your content is performing. Treat the extension as a new negotiation. Review the original usage fee, consider the content's proven performance, and propose a renewal rate. Many creators charge 60-75% of the original usage fee for renewals since the production cost is already covered." },
